WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.912
AI agents are starting to spend real money:
booking

2
00:00:02.912 --> 00:00:05.652
travel, renewing software, and paying
suppliers.

3
00:00:05.892 --> 00:00:09.709
That creates a hard security problem. An agent
can be prompt-injected,

4
00:00:09.709 --> 00:00:13.416
its credential can leak, or it can simply make
an expensive mistake.

5
00:00:13.656 --> 00:00:17.566
And the same model that was manipulated should
not be trusted to approve its own payment.

6
00:00:17.806 --> 00:00:21.079
I built AgentGuard for the Razorpay AI
Buildathon to put a reliable

7
00:00:21.079 --> 00:00:23.667
guardrail between an autonomous agent and real
money.

8
00:00:23.907 --> 00:00:28.323
Every payment request is authenticated,
checked against deterministic organization

9
00:00:28.323 --> 00:00:32.577
policy, scored for behavioral risk, and
enriched with relevant policy evidence.

10
00:00:32.817 --> 00:00:35.580
The result is one of three explainable
decisions:

11
00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.779
allow, send for human review, or block.

12
00:00:38.019 --> 00:00:41.789
The core rule is simple: AI provides
intelligence, but deterministic

13
00:00:41.789 --> 00:00:44.061
security policies retain final authority.

14
00:00:44.301 --> 00:00:47.043
The AI layer may escalate risk, but it can
never downgrade

15
00:00:47.043 --> 00:00:49.643
a review or release a payment that a hard rule
blocked.

16
00:00:49.883 --> 00:00:53.062
Here is the working application. I will sign
in with the seeded

17
00:00:53.062 --> 00:00:55.939
demo administrator and enter the isolated test
workspace.

18
00:00:56.179 --> 00:01:00.984
The dashboard summarizes real records from
MongoDB: active agents, autonomous

19
00:01:00.984 --> 00:01:05.727
approvals, review requests, blocks, risk
distribution, and recent decisions.

20
00:01:05.967 --> 00:01:09.169
AgentGuard is designed to make payment
governance understandable at

21
00:01:09.169 --> 00:01:12.084
a glance, without hiding the reasoning behind
a single score.

22
00:01:12.324 --> 00:01:16.187
Each agent is attached to an enforcing policy
with per-transaction,

23
00:01:16.187 --> 00:01:19.762
daily, and monthly limits, permitted
categories, vendor rules,

24
00:01:19.762 --> 00:01:22.760
geography controls, and repeated-failure
thresholds.

25
00:01:22.1000 --> 00:01:26.709
Alongside those deterministic rules, policy
documents are cleaned,

26
00:01:26.709 --> 00:01:29.744
chunked, embedded, and indexed for semantic
retrieval.

27
00:01:29.984 --> 00:01:33.520
This vendor policy says that newly registered
or unverified vendors

28
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.475
require human approval, regardless of
transaction value.

29
00:01:36.715 --> 00:01:41.606
First, a normal transaction. The Subscription
Bot requests four hundred and ninety-nine
rupees

30
00:01:41.606 --> 00:01:46.030
for Adobe, a known software vendor with
successful payment history in this workspace.

31
00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:49.613
The purpose and agent intent are included so
AgentGuard can evaluate

32
00:01:49.613 --> 00:01:52.613
both structured transaction signals and the
business context.

33
00:01:52.853 --> 00:01:56.925
The policy permits the category, the amount is
comfortably inside its limits,

34
00:01:56.925 --> 00:02:00.416
and the merchant is trusted. The decision is
allow, with low risk.

35
00:02:00.656 --> 00:02:05.288
Only after that approval does the backend
create a Razorpay order. This is the real
Razorpay

36
00:02:05.288 --> 00:02:09.115
checkout in Test Mode; no production
credentials or real funds are involved.

37
00:02:09.355 --> 00:02:13.565
The browser receives only Razorpay's public
test checkout data. Payment signatures

38
00:02:13.565 --> 00:02:17.312
are verified server-side before any
transaction can be marked successful.

39
00:02:17.552 --> 00:02:23.329
Back in AgentGuard, the transaction view shows
the full decision pipeline: agent
authentication,

40
00:02:23.329 --> 00:02:27.661
guardrails, policy retrieval, RAG analysis,
and the final risk decision.

41
00:02:27.901 --> 00:02:31.233
It also explains the risk signals, every
deterministic check,

42
00:02:31.233 --> 00:02:34.401
the selected policy version, and the Razorpay
order state.

43
00:02:34.641 --> 00:02:39.313
Now I will try suspicious behavior using the
Procurement Agent. This is a
nine-thousand-rupee

44
00:02:39.313 --> 00:02:41.976
payment to a new supplier with no successful
history.

45
00:02:42.216 --> 00:02:44.515
The amount itself is within the policy limit,
but

46
00:02:44.515 --> 00:02:46.768
the vendor trust signal is deliberately
missing.

47
00:02:47.008 --> 00:02:50.885
AgentGuard does not blindly reject the
purchase. It returns review, keeps

48
00:02:50.885 --> 00:02:54.390
payment as not initiated, and routes the case
to a human approver.

49
00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:57.512
The reason codes identify the exact controls:
the vendor

50
00:02:57.512 --> 00:02:59.674
is unverified and the merchant is unknown.

51
00:02:59.914 --> 00:03:03.247
The explanation includes policy evidence
retrieved specifically

52
00:03:03.247 --> 00:03:05.998
for this workspace, together with similarity
scores.

53
00:03:06.238 --> 00:03:10.789
The RAG analysis uses that evidence to explain
why approval is required. Retrieved

54
00:03:10.789 --> 00:03:15.229
text is evidence, not executable instruction,
so it cannot override a hard rule.

55
00:03:15.469 --> 00:03:18.706
Next I repeat the same pending payment. The
agent, merchant,

56
00:03:18.706 --> 00:03:20.864
and amount match a recent authorization.

57
00:03:21.104 --> 00:03:24.613
AgentGuard detects the replay and blocks it
with the duplicate payment

58
00:03:24.613 --> 00:03:27.720
reason code. Razorpay is not contacted for the
second attempt.

59
00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:30.814
This is a deterministic hard stop, so the
language model is

60
00:03:30.814 --> 00:03:33.522
skipped and cannot talk the system into
releasing funds.

61
00:03:33.762 --> 00:03:37.141
Under the hood, the frontend is React nineteen
with Vite, and

62
00:03:37.141 --> 00:03:40.355
the API is FastAPI with Pydantic and
asynchronous PyMongo.

63
00:03:40.595 --> 00:03:45.283
Authentication uses signed JWT sessions and
bcrypt passwords. Agent credentials

64
00:03:45.283 --> 00:03:48.844
are hashed, revocable, and bound to a single
agent identity.

65
00:03:49.084 --> 00:03:52.664
RAG retrieval is workspace-scoped, using
MongoDB Atlas vector search

66
00:03:52.664 --> 00:03:56.140
when available and a local cosine-search
fallback for development.

67
00:03:56.380 --> 00:04:00.761
The payment layer creates Razorpay orders only
for allowed or human-approved decisions,

68
00:04:00.761 --> 00:04:04.790
then verifies checkout signatures and signed,
idempotent webhooks on the server.

69
00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:09.033
Finally, the audit log records server-side
events for authorization

70
00:04:09.033 --> 00:04:12.975
requests, policy outcomes, approval changes,
and payment activity.

71
00:04:13.215 --> 00:04:17.328
That gives developers and reviewers a
traceable answer to who requested a payment,

72
00:04:17.328 --> 00:04:20.739
what policy ran, why the decision happened,
and whether money moved.

73
00:04:20.979 --> 00:04:23.860
AgentGuard helps autonomous systems spend
safely without

74
00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:26.330
removing useful autonomy from legitimate
agents.

75
00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:30.772
It combines deterministic controls,
explainable risk, policy-grounded AI

76
00:04:30.772 --> 00:04:34.742
reasoning, human review, and Razorpay Test
Mode in one working flow.

77
00:04:34.982 --> 00:04:39.473
AI agents get intelligence. They do not get
unrestricted authority. Thank you.
